---
title: "process_certificate (Sui Whitepaper: Authority Base Operation)"
---
stateDiagram-v2
    direction TB
    [*] --> q_validate: receive TxCert from client
    q_validate --> q_check_cert_locks: all validity conditions pass (except lock checks)
    q_validate --> q_error_invalid: validity check failed
    q_error_invalid --> [*]: return error

    q_check_cert_locks --> q_check_shared: Lock_v[ObjRef] is None, TxSign, or cert for same Tx
    q_check_cert_locks --> q_disaster: Lock_v[ObjRef] set to cert for different Tx
    q_disaster --> [*]: halt and start disaster recovery (Byzantine failure detected)

    q_check_shared --> q_execute: no shared objects OR shared objects sequenced by consensus
    q_check_shared --> q_wait_consensus: shared objects not yet sequenced
    q_wait_consensus --> q_check_shared: consensus sequences the certificate

    q_execute --> q_store_cert: exec(Tx, [Obj]) produces Effects
    q_store_cert --> q_update_locks: Ct_v[TxDigest] = (TxCert, EffSign)
    q_update_locks --> q_materialize: Lock_v[ObjRef] = TxCert for all owned inputs
    q_materialize --> q_update_sync: add ObjRef and contents to Obj_v
    q_update_sync --> [*]: update Sync_v[ObjRef] = TxDigest, return EffSign

    note right of q_check_cert_locks
        INV-001: Two contradictory certificates for same lock is proof of Byzantine failure
        INV-002: Causal execution order - authority processes cert only if all input objects exist
        INV-003: Execution is deterministic and idempotent
    end note
